(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-pdm-exam-1-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form E","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A patient has a lesion you suspect is a neoplasm, or a rash that has not resolved. Which test follows?","choices":["Gram stain of a surface swab","Biopsy","Potassium hydroxide preparation or culture","Point-of-care ultrasound"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — neoplasm or a persistent rash routes to biopsy. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does an increased mean platelet volume represent, and what lowers it?","choices":["An increase in mature platelets, as in polycythemia; lowered by recent blood loss","Platelet clumping in the sample; lowered by prolonged storage","An increase in platelet number; lowered by a decrease in number","An increase in immature platelets, as after recent blood loss; lowered by bone marrow failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the raised and lowered readings pair this way. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"The blood pad is positive. What can it NOT tell you?","choices":["Whether the specimen was fresh","Whether heme is present at all","Which source is responsible","Whether protein is also present"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the pad does not differentiate between them. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which of these lowers the specific gravity through the kidney's own failure to concentrate?","choices":["Hypotension","Renal artery stenosis","Chronic kidney disease","Heart failure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — glomerulonephritis, tubular damage, and renal failure all cost concentrating ability. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How do the kidneys handle potassium?","choices":["It is neither filtered nor secreted, and is lost only in stool","It is reabsorbed in exchange for sodium in the proximal tubule","It is excreted by the kidneys with no reabsorption","It is filtered and then almost entirely reabsorbed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — excreted with no reabsorption. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What does the Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments program establish?","choices":["Federal guidelines for minimum quality standards when testing human samples at all types of sites","Voluntary best-practice recommendations that apply to hospital laboratories but not to other sites","Maximum permissible turnaround times for laboratory results","Reimbursement rates for laboratory testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it sets the floor, and it arose from problems in cytology laboratories reading Papanicolaou smears. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which of the following is listed as a variable that can affect laboratory results?","choices":["Whether the test was ordered electronically","Time of specimen collection","The patient's insurance carrier","The ordering clinician's specialty"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The list also includes patient preparation, current drug therapy, physical activity, hydration, age, sex and body mass index. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does ketonuria indicate about how cells are producing energy?","choices":["They are metabolising glucose instead of fatty acids","They are metabolising protein instead of carbohydrate","They are metabolising fatty acids instead of glucose","They have stopped producing energy altogether"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — ketones are the by-product of that switch. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What is the standard formula for the anion gap?","choices":["Sodium minus the sum of chloride and bicarbonate","Sodium minus chloride, without subtracting bicarbonate","The sum of sodium and potassium minus chloride","Chloride plus bicarbonate minus sodium"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the standard formula. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What does the blood pad on a reagent strip actually detect?","choices":["Iron released from ferritin stores","Heme","Intact red cells only","Free hemoglobin only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and heme is present in all three of the possible sources. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is first-line for a neck mass, and what question does it answer?","choices":["Ultrasound, distinguishing superficial or cystic from solid","Contrast computed tomography, distinguishing abscess from phlegmon","Magnetic resonance imaging, distinguishing benign from malignant","Fine needle aspiration, distinguishing reactive from neoplastic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — lymphadenopathy and thyroid are the usual examples. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What do basophil granules contain?","choices":["Histamines only","Enzymes that digest bacterial cell walls","Denatured hemoglobin and residual nuclear material","Heparin, histamine and other mediators of inflammation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — heparin is what distinguishes basophil granules from eosinophil ones. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A patient has a white cell count of 2,000 per microlitre with 20% neutrophils and no bands. What is the absolute neutrophil count, and how is the neutropenia categorised?","choices":["4,000 per microlitre, which is a normal count","200 per microlitre, which is severe neutropenia","400 per microlitre, which is severe neutropenia","400 per microlitre, which is moderate neutropenia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — 2,000 x 20 / 100 = 400, and severe is below 500 cells per microlitre. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 21 (image only)"},{"q":"Why is bicarbonate reported on a chemistry panel as carbon dioxide?","choices":["The panel reports total carbon dioxide, which is mostly serum bicarbonate","The laboratory measures dissolved carbon dioxide gas directly","Bicarbonate is unstable and must be converted before measurement","It reflects the carbon dioxide cleared by the lungs that minute"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the panel reports total carbon dioxide. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Why is creatinine a more specific marker of kidney function than blood urea nitrogen?","choices":["It rises earlier in kidney injury than any other marker","It is filtered by the kidneys and rises as the filtration rate falls, without the many non-renal influences on urea nitrogen","It is produced at a rate that never varies between people, whatever their build or age","It is measured by a more accurate laboratory method"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — specificity is the contrast between the two. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What equipment does fluoroscopy require?","choices":["An x-ray unit fitted for controlled motion of the source, the imaging sensor and the patient, with a tilting table","A gamma camera mounted on a rotating gantry that circles the patient, with a fixed table and a detector housing that does not move","A superconducting magnet requiring dedicated site construction beforehand and high ongoing cost","A handheld transducer connected to a portable processing unit, needing neither a tilting table nor a shielded examination room"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the tube moves freely back and forth to image the patient. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A urine pregnancy test returns simply positive or negative. How is this analyser type classified?","choices":["Multiple-use benchtop","Qualitative","Quantitative","Semi-quantitative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — rapid strep and influenza tests are likewise qualitative; a urinalysis dipstick is semi-quantitative. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What concentration of potassium hydroxide does the preparation use?","choices":["Ten per cent","Five per cent","Forty per cent","Twenty per cent"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — one drop of twenty per cent potassium hydroxide is added to the specimen. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which two of the five basic densities cannot be distinguished from one another on a conventional radiograph?","choices":["Fluid and soft tissue, which have the same density","Fat and air, which both appear black on the image","Calcium and metal, which both absorb all of the beam","Fat and calcium, which occupy adjacent points on the scale"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that limitation is one reason computed tomography expands the grey scale. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which of the following is a named quality assurance measure for point-of-care testing?","choices":["Annual replacement of every device regardless of its measured performance","Testing only specimens collected by laboratory staff","Reporting results verbally rather than electronically","Active enrollment and participation in an External Quality Assurance program"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside trained and competent operators, correct specimen collection, accurate patient identification and quality control. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What does a healthy optic disc look like?","choices":["An enlarged, deeply excavated cup with an undermined rim","A pale disc with sharply defined margins and no cup","A cup filling more than two-thirds of the disc diameter","A small central cup with a robust neuroretinal rim"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that is the healthy disc. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which findings make sinus or orbital imaging an emergency?","choices":["Nasal obstruction, anosmia and postnasal drip","Hearing loss, tinnitus and vertigo","Facial swelling, proptosis, eye signs or neurologic signs","Fever, purulent discharge, facial pain and headache"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — those four findings make imaging an emergency. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which complication follows BOTH high and low potassium?","choices":["Seizures","Acute kidney injury","Hepatic encephalopathy with confusion","Life-threatening cardiac arrhythmias"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — arrhythmia is the shared danger at either extreme. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 10 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which test is listed among common point-of-care tests in the acute care setting?","choices":["Troponin","Hemoglobin A1c","Fecal occult blood","Cholesterol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, alongside venous blood gas, point-of-care glucose, brain natriuretic peptide and D-dimer. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Why must the timing of a reagent strip reading be watched closely?","choices":["All pads must be read at exactly sixty seconds","Each analyte has its own reading time","The strip must be read before it touches the urine","Reading time only matters for the pH pad"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the times differ from one pad to the next. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 36 (image)"},{"q":"Why does understanding a patient's perceptions and anxieties about testing matter?","choices":["It identifies which patients should be referred to another clinician before any testing begins","It allows communication on a deeper level so that a therapeutic relationship can develop","It allows the clinician to decide whether to disclose the result","It determines whether consent is legally required"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Empathy is expected throughout all phases of testing. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What must be checked before giving intravenous iodinated contrast, and why?","choices":["Liver function tests and bilirubin, because the agent is cleared hepatically","Thyroid function tests, because the iodine load can precipitate thyrotoxicosis","Complete blood count and platelets, because the agent affects coagulation","Blood urea nitrogen and creatinine, because the agent is nephrotoxic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and a litre of normal saline is given to protect the kidneys. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What defines the agranulocytes, and which cells are they?","choices":["No distinctive granules and a non-lobular nucleus, comprising eosinophils and basophils","Granules that do not stain, comprising neutrophils and monocytes","No distinctive granules and a non-lobular nucleus, comprising monocytes and lymphocytes","No distinctive granules and a lobed nucleus, comprising monocytes and lymphocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both defining features and both cell types. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"In which units is radiation measured?","choices":["Tesla and gauss","Hertz and decibels","Millisieverts and milligrays","Hounsfield units and pixels"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — x-ray radiation is low compared with other radiographic modalities. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Urine is noted to be foamy. Which finding does that suggest?","choices":["Proteinuria","Bilirubinuria","Glucosuria","Ketonuria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — foam is associated with protein in the urine. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Why does a left shift occur during an acute bacterial infection?","choices":["Bacteria selectively destroy mature neutrophils in the tissue","Neutrophils are increasingly consumed, so the marrow releases both mature and immature forms to keep up","The marrow is suppressed, so only immature forms can be produced","Immature cells survive considerably longer in the circulation than the fully mature ones do"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is the mechanism. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Which alternative absolute neutrophil count formula is used when the white cell count is expressed in thousands?","choices":["Ten multiplied by the white cell count in thousands, multiplied by the percentage of neutrophils plus bands","One hundred multiplied by the white cell count in thousands, multiplied by the percentage of neutrophils plus bands","The white cell count in thousands divided by the percentage of neutrophils plus bands","The white cell count in thousands multiplied by the percentage of neutrophils, with bands excluded"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the second of the two equivalent standard forms. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 20 (image only)"},{"q":"What are the formulas for mean corpuscular hemoglobin and mean corpuscular hemoglobin concentration?","choices":["Mean corpuscular hemoglobin is hemoglobin times ten over the red cell count; the concentration is hemoglobin times one hundred over hematocrit","Mean corpuscular hemoglobin is hemoglobin times one hundred over hematocrit; the concentration is hemoglobin times ten over the red cell count","Both are hemoglobin times ten over the red cell count, differing only in units","Both are calculated from hematocrit and the red cell count, without using hemoglobin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — both from the formula images, and the denominator is what tells them apart. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54 (image only)"},{"q":"Besides urea nitrogen and creatinine, what else appears in the renal pattern?","choices":["A raised bilirubin with a lowered alkaline phosphatase","A raised phosphate, a lowered calcium, and albuminuria","A raised calcium, a lowered phosphate, and glycosuria","A raised albumin with a lowered total protein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — these sit alongside the potassium and the acidosis. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A patient is found after several hours trapped under a fallen beam. The dipstick is positive for blood but no red cells are seen. Which test confirms the source?","choices":["A twenty-four hour urine protein collection","Serum unconjugated bilirubin","Urine culture and sensitivity","Serum creatine phosphokinase"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a raised level points to muscle as the source. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"In the iron comparison table, what happens to transferrin and total iron binding capacity in IRON DEFICIENCY?","choices":["Both stay normal, while transferrin saturation falls","Both rise, while transferrin saturation also rises","Both rise, while transferrin saturation falls","Both fall, while transferrin saturation rises"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — more carrier, less cargo, so saturation drops. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63 (image only)"},{"q":"Which disadvantages are named for conventional radiography?","choices":["Operator dependence and an inability to penetrate bone or large gas-filled structures","Long scan times and safety problems with magnetic implanted devices","Very high radiation dose and an inability to be made portable at all","A limited range of densities and reliance on ionizing radiation, though at relatively low dose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the dose is low compared with other radiographic modalities. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which study is used for deep neck infection, and what is the role of ultrasound there?","choices":["Plain radiography of the soft tissues of the neck, with ultrasound if negative","Contrast computed tomography of the neck, and it states that ultrasound is not helpful","Ultrasound of the neck first, escalating to computed tomography if inconclusive","Magnetic resonance imaging of the neck, with ultrasound as a screening step"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — and ultrasound is not helpful for this indication. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which additional elements does a microscopic urinalysis report?","choices":["Red cells, white cells, epithelial cells, casts, and crystals","Only bacteria and yeast","Only casts and crystals","The same nine analytes as the dipstick, measured precisely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — those are the sediment findings. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"To what depth must a melanoma biopsy extend, and why?","choices":["Deep enough to include the regional lymphatics, so nodal spread can be excluded","Deep enough to avoid transecting the base, so Breslow depth can be measured","Deep enough to reach subcutaneous fat, so the Clark level can be assigned","Into the papillary dermis only, so the wound heals without a graft"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a transected base destroys the single most important prognostic measurement. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient with sweet-smelling urine has ketones and glucose on the dipstick. Which picture does that fit?","choices":["Uncontrolled diabetes mellitus","Rhabdomyolysis","Biliary obstruction","A simple urinary tract infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — glucose spilling with ketones from fatty acid metabolism. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What does the colour Doppler setting add to a standard ultrasound image?","choices":["Density in Hounsfield units and windowing, on an image otherwise showing five densities","Metabolic uptake and intensity, on an image otherwise showing only anatomical structure","Direction of flow and velocity, on an image otherwise made of white, grey and black","Depth of penetration and resolution, on an image otherwise limited to superficial planes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — colour is the flow overlay, and blood is what it is used for. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Which factors should be considered when communicating about diagnostic testing?","choices":["Only whether an interpreter is required","Only the urgency of the clinical situation","Ethnicity, culture, gender and age","Only the patient's stated educational level"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — these are named alongside preparing the patient properly and following standards. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Below what platelet count does the risk of hemorrhage increase?","choices":["Twenty thousand","Fifty thousand","One hundred thousand","One hundred and fifty thousand"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — that is the threshold for increased hemorrhage risk. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"What practical ordering point applies when imaging several body regions?","choices":["Multiple body parts require the radiologist to countersign the request before booking","Multiple body parts must be imaged on separate days to limit cumulative radiation dose","Multiple body parts or sections may mean multiple orders, giving brain, cervical, thoracic and lumbar spine as the example","Multiple body parts should be combined into one single order, so that the patient is scanned once and the radiologist reads the whole study together"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — one clinical question can require four separate requests. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What causes INCREASED platelets?","choices":["Bone marrow failure, acute hemorrhage and polycythemia vera","Trauma, acute hemorrhage, iron deficiency and polycythemia vera","Trauma, chemotherapy, alcohol and aplastic anemia","Radiation, drugs, alcohol and iron deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — note that iron deficiency raises platelets while lowering red cells. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the window in computed tomography?","choices":["The physical aperture of the gantry through which the patient passes","The interval during which the contrast sits at its peak concentration in the vessel","The slice thickness selected before the scan is acquired","The range of Hounsfield numbers pre-selected for display, spread over the available grey scale"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — for example only those densities between −100 and +300. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which is described as the highest-volume point-of-care test using a single-use quantitative cartridge or strip with a reader?","choices":["Glucose","Rapid strep","Urinalysis dipstick","Urine pregnancy testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — listed alongside blood chemistries, coagulation studies, cardiac markers and hemoglobin A1c. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What determines the frequency of the radio waves a magnetic resonance scanner reads?","choices":["The metabolic rate of the tissue, together with the dose of tracer given","The chemical environment of the hydrogen atoms, together with their location","The strength of the magnet in Tesla, together with the duration of the scan","The density of the tissue in Hounsfield units, together with the chosen window"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both, which is what lets the computer build a spatial map. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which activity belongs to the intratest phase?","choices":["Identifying contraindications to the test","Ordering appropriate follow-up laboratory studies","Assessing the patient's coping styles, fears and phobias before the procedure is scheduled","Monitoring vital signs and administering analgesics or sedatives during the procedure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the intratest phase covers performing the test and everything it encompasses. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How does the lung dose of a lateral chest radiograph compare with the posterior-anterior view?","choices":["It is lower, which is one reason the lateral view is taken first in a standard series","It is identical, because both views expose the same volume of lung tissue","It is higher, but only in children, because the chest wall is thinner","It is higher, which is one reason the posterior-anterior view is the preferred projection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the posterior-anterior view also delivers a lower dose to sensitive organs. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which four core tests evaluate water and electrolyte balance?","choices":["Serum sodium, serum potassium, serum calcium, serum magnesium, and serum phosphate","Serum glucose, bicarbonate, the anion gap, and ketones","Albumin, total protein, prothrombin time, and bilirubin","Serum sodium, serum osmolality, urine sodium and osmolality, and the urea nitrogen to creatinine ratio"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — that core set, with potassium alongside. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A patient with recurrent stones has persistently alkaline urine and repeated infections. Which stone type does that combination suggest?","choices":["Uric acid","Struvite","Calcium oxalate","Cystine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — alkaline urine with urease-producing organisms. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A patient's reduced acuity corrects with a pinhole. What does that indicate?","choices":["Ocular pathology is likely present","The optic nerve is likely involved","The result is uninterpretable without formal refraction","A refractive error is the likely cause"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — correcting with a pinhole points to refraction rather than disease. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which four non-renal factors raise blood urea nitrogen?","choices":["Dehydration, haemolysis, low protein intake, and pregnancy","Liver failure, malnutrition, overhydration, and immobility","Muscle injury, strenuous exercise, high creatine intake, and male sex","Dehydration, gastrointestinal bleeding, high protein intake, and catabolic states"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — those four, which is why the value is non-specific. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why is a tongue blade used during a throat culture?","choices":["To keep the patient from swallowing during collection","To protect the clinician from exposure","To increase visualization of the pharynx, relax the throat muscles and decrease the gag reflex","To collect the specimen directly from the surface of the tongue where organisms accumulate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — all three purposes are named. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which three computed tomography findings indicate sinus infection?","choices":["Bone erosion, gas within soft tissue and perineural spread","Mucosal thickening, air-fluid levels and sinus opacification","Fat stranding, abscess and herniated orbital contents","Rim-enhancing fluid collection and enlarged or necrotic lymph nodes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the three sinus findings listed. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What are the limitations of superficial wound swabs?","choices":["They take twenty-four to forty-eight hours, and are operator dependent","They require an antimicrobial-free cleaning step, and are uncomfortable for most patients","They are prone to contamination and colonisation, and may not correlate well with deep infection","They are prone to contamination, and cannot provide susceptibilities"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both limitations as stated. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A chemistry panel is best described as a snapshot of which three things?","choices":["Oxygen delivery, oxygen consumption, and tissue perfusion","Nutrition, hydration, and body composition","Chemical balance, metabolism, and organ function","Infection, inflammation, and immune response"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — chemical balance, metabolism and organ function. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"How does loss of chloride, as in vomiting, produce a metabolic alkalosis?","choices":["It raises the strong ion difference","It lowers the strong ion difference","It directly removes hydrogen ions from the blood","It stimulates the lungs to retain carbon dioxide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it raises the strong ion difference. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
